---
title: Apple Cider Brined Turkey
course: main
cuisine: american
servings: 10
time_active: 60
time_total: 1260
leftovers: great
tags: [holiday, make-ahead]
pairs: [turkey-gravy, bread-stuffing]
description: An 11–12 lb turkey brined overnight in apple cider, citrus, and herbs, then roasted.
---

> Don't brine a kosher, self-basting, or "enhanced" turkey; it's already salted. A frozen bird needs about 3 days in the fridge to thaw.

In a large #pot{}, bring @water{6%cups} to a simmer with @kosher salt{1 1/2%cups}(Diamond Crystal, or 3/4 cup Morton), @brown sugar{1/2%cup}, @black peppercorns{1 1/2%tbsp}, @?allspice berries{1 1/2%tbsp}, @bay leaves{4}, @fresh rosemary{4%sprigs}, @fresh thyme{4%sprigs}, and @fresh sage{4%sprigs}. Stir until the salt and sugar dissolve, then take it off the heat.

Add @water{14%cups}(cold, or partly ice) and @apple cider{4%cups}. Stir in @orange{1}(sliced), @lemon{1}(sliced), @apple{1}(quartered), @yellow onion{1}(quartered), @garlic{1%head}(cut crosswise), @carrots{3}(roughly chopped), and @celery{3%stalks}(roughly chopped). Chill until fully cold.

Remove the neck and giblets from the @turkey{11-12%lb} and save them for the gravy. Submerge the turkey in the cold brine in a #brining bag{} or 5-gallon bucket and refrigerate ~{12-18%hours}.

Lift the turkey out, discard the brine, and pat it very dry. For crisp skin, leave it uncovered in the fridge for a few hours or overnight.

Heat the oven to 325°F. Scatter @yellow onion{1}(chopped), @carrot{1}(chopped), and @celery{1%stalk}(chopped) in a #roasting pan{} with a rack, add @chicken stock{1%cup}(low-sodium), and set the turkey on the rack.

Roast until the thickest part of the breast reads 160°F and the thigh 175°F, ~{2 1/2-3%hours}. Add a splash of water if the pan goes dry. Tent with foil and rest ~{30-40%minutes} while you make the gravy.

> Brining makes the drippings salty, so season the gravy only after tasting.
